  "strategy": "I propose a novel algorithm based on Bayesian optimization with a multi-point random Fourier basis representation and a thresholding scheme to minimize the evaluation function over sparsified subsets of the search space.\n\nInsight-based approach:\nThe evaluation function measures imbalance between sequence length (n) and convolution peak height (max_b). We achieve lower costs by:\n- Representing candidate sequences as weighted sums of orthonormal basis functions\n- Iteratively removing basis components that contribute negatively to optimization\n- Maintaining a reserved set of core solution components\n\nAlgorithm specifics:\n\n1. Initialization with Fourier basis (handled by first call to initial sequence interpolation)\n   - Start with parent sequence extended to full 2^n harmonics\n   - All coefficients weighted equally\n\n2. Core optimization loop:\n   - Sample candidate sequence lengths uniformly from [4, 2560]\n   - For each candidate length n:\n        * Initialize with a fresh Fourier basis representation scaled by 0.6\n        * Compute gradient from known convolution formula\n        * Retain only top-k basis components (k=active_constraint_count)\n   - Update heldout basis components persistently\n\n3. Optimization goal:\n   Minimize score = (2 * n * conv_max) / sum_squared\n\nImplementation plan:\n\nThe function will iteratively compute:\n- Fourier series coefficients\n- Convolution peaks\n- Gradient component sums\n- Active constraint thresholds\n\nEach iteration adds short debugging consistent in style.\n\nMaximum iteration count: 5000\nTime budget: 1800s\nAutomatic termination triggered by excessive candidate evaluations\n\nFinally, the validator measures a specific convolution and objective value.",
